INT. TRAIN COMPARTMENT - NIGHT

A first-class compartment, 1938. Plush seats, brass fittings, a lamp with a fringed shade. The wheels beat out a steady rhythm.

A young woman in a dark traveling suit sits very straight with a small suitcase on her knees. Across from her, a man in his forties in a gray overcoat sits behind a newspaper, the paper lowering by degrees.

STRANGER
Forgive me. You're traveling alone?

CLAUDINE
I am.

STRANGER
A long way to go alone.

CLAUDINE
I have been going a long way for some time.

He folds the newspaper and sets it aside. She keeps her hands on the suitcase.

STRANGER
You'll pardon an old man's curiosity. Your gloves. They're a man's.

CLAUDINE
They were my father's.

STRANGER
And where is your father?

CLAUDINE
That is the question I am traveling to answer.

He studies her. The lamp sways.

STRANGER
Then perhaps I can help you.

CLAUDINE
Why would you?

STRANGER
(a thin smile)
Because I knew him.

She does not move. The suitcase lid trembles under her fingers.

CLAUDINE
What was his name?

STRANGER
Anton Rieux.

CLAUDINE
And yours?

STRANGER
Mine?

CLAUDINE
You know his name. I would like to know yours.

STRANGER
(after a long pause)
Reynard.

CLAUDINE
Mr. Reynard.

REYNARD
Just Reynard.

CLAUDINE
Then, Reynard, tell me where he is.

REYNARD
Mademoiselle. He is in the next car.

The train rattles through a tunnel. The lamp flickers out, and for three seconds there is only the sound of wheels. When it flares back, Claudine has opened the suitcase. Her hand rests on what is inside it.
